= Context (linguistics) =

== Overview ==
In linguistics, '''context''' is the information or circumstances relevant to interpreting a linguistic expression or utterance.

“Context” does not name one single variable. It can include surrounding language, speaker and addressee, time and place, discourse state, perceptual surroundings, social setting, goals, and mutually recognized assumptions.

== Linguistic context ==
'''Linguistic context''' or '''co-text''' is the language surrounding an expression.

For example, a pronoun such as “she” may depend on an earlier noun phrase for interpretation.

Co-text is important, but:

<pre>
co-text ≠ all context
</pre>

== Extralinguistic context ==
Interpretation can also depend on facts outside the surrounding text, such as:

* who is speaking;
* when and where an utterance occurs;
* what objects are perceptually available;
* social roles and institutions;
* shared activity and goals.

Indexicals such as “I”, “here”, and “now” depend directly on such circumstances.

== Context and common ground ==
[[Common ground]] is information or commitments treated as mutually available among participants.

It is one important model of conversational context, but it is not identical to context as a whole.

A visible object, time of utterance, institutional role, or linguistic antecedent can affect interpretation without being modeled simply as a shared proposition.

== Context in semantics and pragmatics ==
[[Semantics]] and [[Pragmatics]] use different notions of context.

Formal semantics may model a context as a tuple containing parameters such as speaker, time, place, and world.

Dynamic semantics may model context as an information state that utterances update.

Pragmatics may require broader social, intentional, and discourse information.

These are related abstractions, not interchangeable definitions.

== Why context matters ==
Context helps explain:

* reference and deixis;
* anaphora;
* ellipsis;
* ambiguity resolution;
* presupposition;
* implicature;
* speech acts;
* discourse coherence;
* nonliteral language.

== Scope limits ==
Calling something “context-dependent” does not explain it by itself. A useful analysis must identify which contextual information matters and how it changes interpretation.

